**✅Bubble sort:**

Bubble sort is a simple,comparision -based sorting algorithm that arranges a list of elements by repeatedly comparing adjecent pairs
and swapping them if they are in wrong order.

**✅How it works:**

1. Compare adjecent pairs: Start at the beginning of the list and compare the first two elements.

2. Swap if out of order: If you are sorting in ascending order and the first element is largeer than the second, swap them.

3. Move forward: Move to the next pair of elements and repeat the comparision.

4. Complete the pass: Continiue until you reach the end of the un-sorted portion of the list. By the end of the first pass, the largest
   element will have 'bubbled-up' to its correct final position at the very end.

5. Repeat : Repeat the entire process for the remaining unsorted elements until a full pass occurs without a single swap, indicating the
   list is completely sorted.

**✅Example:-    [5,1,4]**

*First Pass :-

compare 5 and 1, 5>1, swap them - [1,5,4].
compare 5 and 4. 5>4, swap them - [1,4,5].

Result :- The largest element (5) has bubbled to the end.

*Second Pass:-

compare 1 and 4. They are in the correct order so no swap.

Result :- The algorithm finishes the pass with no swaps, meaning the array is successfully sorted.


**✅Core Technical Characteristics:-**

*Time Complexity : It has a worst case and average time complexity of O(n^2) due to its nested loops, making it highly inefficient for large 
                   datasets. When optimized with swaptracking flag,its best-case time complexity is O(n) of the data is already sorted.

*Space Complexity : It is an in-place algorithm of O(1), requiring no extra memory storage to complete the sort.

*Stability : It is a stable sorting algorithm, meaning elements with equal values retain their orignal relative order.
